  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t1",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A charge of 120 C flows through a wire in one minute. The current is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "2 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "7200 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "0.5 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "120 A"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "I = Q/t = 120 C / 60 s = 2 A."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q02",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t1",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A wire of resistance R is stretched to twice its length, its volume staying the same. Its new resistance is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "R"
   },
   {
    "en": "4R"
   },
   {
    "en": "R/2"
   },
   {
    "en": "2R"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "Doubling the length halves the area (same volume). R = ρL/A becomes ρ(2L)/(A/2) = 4R."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q03",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t1",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A heater is rated 1150 W, 230 V. Its resistance is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "5 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "46 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "0.2 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "230 Ω"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "R = V²/P = 230²/1150 = 52900/1150 = 46 Ω (current 5 A)."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q04",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t1",
  "stem": {
   "en": "The SI unit of conductance is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "siemens"
   },
   {
    "en": "weber"
   },
   {
    "en": "ohm"
   },
   {
    "en": "henry"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "Conductance G = 1/R is measured in siemens (S), the reciprocal of the ohm."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q05",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t1",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A 2 kW geyser runs for 30 minutes every day. The energy used in 30 days is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "60 kWh"
   },
   {
    "en": "15 kWh"
   },
   {
    "en": "3600 kWh"
   },
   {
    "en": "30 kWh"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "Energy per day = 2 kW × 0.5 h = 1 kWh; for 30 days = 30 kWh."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q06",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t2",
  "stem": {
   "en": "Three 6 Ω resistors are connected in parallel. The equivalent resistance is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "6 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "18 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "3 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "2 Ω"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "n equal resistors in parallel: R/n = 6/3 = 2 Ω."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q07",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t2",
  "stem": {
   "en": "The equivalent resistance of 4 Ω and 12 Ω in parallel is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "0.33 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "3 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "16 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "8 Ω"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "R_p = (4 × 12)/(4 + 12) = 48/16 = 3 Ω."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q08",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t2",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A 12 V supply is connected across 2 kΩ and 4 kΩ in series. The voltage across the 4 kΩ resistor is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "8 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "12 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "6 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "4 V"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "Voltage divider: 12 × 4/(2 + 4) = 8 V."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q09",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t2",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A current of 10 A divides between 2 Ω and 8 Ω connected in parallel. The current through the 2 Ω resistor is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "10 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "5 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "8 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "2 A"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 2,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "Current divider: I₂Ω = 10 × 8/(2 + 8) = 8 A. The smaller resistor takes the larger current."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q10",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t2",
  "stem": {
   "en": "Four equal resistors R are first connected in series and then in parallel. The ratio R_series : R_parallel is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "16 : 1"
   },
   {
    "en": "1 : 16"
   },
   {
    "en": "8 : 1"
   },
   {
    "en": "4 : 1"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "R_s = 4R and R_p = R/4, so the ratio is 4R ÷ (R/4) = 16."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q11",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t3",
  "stem": {
   "en": "At a junction, currents of 4 A and 6 A enter and 3 A leaves through one wire. The only other wire carries:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "1 A leaving the junction"
   },
   {
    "en": "7 A entering the junction"
   },
   {
    "en": "7 A leaving the junction"
   },
   {
    "en": "13 A leaving the junction"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 2,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "KCL: 4 + 6 = 3 + I, so I = 7 A leaving."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q12",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t3",
  "stem": {
   "en": "Kirchhoff's voltage law is a consequence of the conservation of:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "momentum"
   },
   {
    "en": "energy"
   },
   {
    "en": "charge"
   },
   {
    "en": "mass"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "The net work done in taking a charge round a closed loop is zero, which is conservation of energy. KCL comes from conservation of charge."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q13",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t3",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A 10 V cell of negligible internal resistance is connected to 2 Ω and 3 Ω in series. The voltage drop across the 3 Ω resistor is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "4 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "10 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "3 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "6 V"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "I = 10/(2 + 3) = 2 A; drop across 3 Ω = 2 × 3 = 6 V. KVL check: 4 + 6 = 10 V."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q14",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t3",
  "stem": {
   "en": "Two sources, 10 V with a 2 Ω series resistor and 8 V with a 2 Ω series resistor, feed a common node connected to the other ends through a third 2 Ω resistor. The current through the third resistor is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "1 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "3 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "2 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "9 A"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "KCL at the node: (10 − V)/2 + (8 − V)/2 = V/2 gives V = 6 V, so the current is 6/2 = 3 A."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q15",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t3",
  "stem": {
   "en": "While applying KVL, moving through a resistor R in the same direction as the current I is taken as:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "a rise equal to the emf"
   },
   {
    "en": "a potential rise, +IR"
   },
   {
    "en": "zero"
   },
   {
    "en": "a potential drop, −IR"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "Current flows from higher to lower potential through a resistor, so moving along the current gives a fall of IR."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q16",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t4",
  "stem": {
   "en": "The internal resistance of an ideal voltage source is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "1 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "equal to the load resistance"
   },
   {
    "en": "infinite"
   },
   {
    "en": "zero"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "An ideal voltage source keeps its terminal voltage constant for any load, which needs zero internal resistance."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q17",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t4",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A 10 V voltage source with 2 Ω series resistance is converted to an equivalent current source. It is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "5 A with 2 Ω in series"
   },
   {
    "en": "5 A with 2 Ω in parallel"
   },
   {
    "en": "10 A with 2 Ω in parallel"
   },
   {
    "en": "20 A with 2 Ω in parallel"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "I = E/r = 10/2 = 5 A, and the same 2 Ω is placed in parallel."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q18",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t4",
  "stem": {
   "en": "Under the condition of maximum power transfer, the efficiency of the circuit is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "25 %"
   },
   {
    "en": "50 %"
   },
   {
    "en": "75 %"
   },
   {
    "en": "100 %"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "When R_L = R_s the same current flows through both, so half the power is lost in the source: efficiency 50 %."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q19",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t4",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A 24 V source has an internal resistance of 6 Ω. The maximum power it can deliver to a load is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "24 W"
   },
   {
    "en": "96 W"
   },
   {
    "en": "144 W"
   },
   {
    "en": "48 W"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "P_max = E²/(4R_s) = 576/24 = 24 W, with R_L = 6 Ω."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q20",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t4",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A cell of emf 6 V and internal resistance 1 Ω is connected to a 5 Ω resistor. The terminal voltage is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "6 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "5 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "4 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "1 V"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "I = 6/(5 + 1) = 1 A; V = E − Ir = 6 − 1 = 5 V."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q21",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t5",
  "stem": {
   "en": "In the superposition theorem, when the effect of one source is found, the other ideal voltage sources are:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "doubled"
   },
   {
    "en": "replaced by open circuits"
   },
   {
    "en": "left unchanged"
   },
   {
    "en": "replaced by short circuits"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 3,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "An ideal voltage source has zero internal resistance, so it is replaced by a short circuit; ideal current sources are opened."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q22",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t5",
  "stem": {
   "en": "The Thevenin equivalent of a linear network is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "a voltage source in series with a resistance"
   },
   {
    "en": "a current source in series with a resistance"
   },
   {
    "en": "a current source in parallel with a resistance"
   },
   {
    "en": "a voltage source in parallel with a resistance"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "Thevenin: V_th in series with R_th. Norton is the current source in parallel with R_N."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q23",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t5",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A network has V_th = 10 V and R_th = 2 Ω. The current in a 3 Ω load is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "0.5 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "3.33 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "2 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "5 A"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 2,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "I_L = V_th/(R_th + R_L) = 10/(2 + 3) = 2 A."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q24",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t5",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A network has V_th = 15 V and R_th = 5 Ω. Its Norton current is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "75 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "0.33 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "3 A"
   },
   {
    "en": "20 A"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 2,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "I_N = V_th/R_th = 15/5 = 3 A, with R_N = 5 Ω."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q25",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t5",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A 24 V source in series with 6 Ω feeds terminals A–B, with 3 Ω connected across A–B. The Thevenin equivalent at A–B is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "24 V in series with 9 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "8 V in series with 2 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "16 V in series with 2 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "8 V in series with 9 Ω"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "V_th = 24 × 3/(6 + 3) = 8 V; R_th = 6 ∥ 3 = 18/9 = 2 Ω."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "tags": []
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q26",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t4",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A battery of emf 12 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω supplies a current of 2.5 A. Its terminal voltage is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "10.75 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "13.25 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "11.5 V"
   },
   {
    "en": "12 V"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "V = E − Ir = 12 − 2.5 × 0.5 = 12 − 1.25 = 10.75 V."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "tags": [
   "board-pattern"
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q27",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t4",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A source of emf 10 V and internal resistance 2 Ω is connected to a variable load. The maximum power it can deliver to the load is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "12.5 W"
   },
   {
    "en": "25 W"
   },
   {
    "en": "50 W"
   },
   {
    "en": "5 W"
   }
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "explanation": {
   "en": "Maximum power is delivered when R_L = r = 2 Ω: P_max = E²/4r = 100/8 = 12.5 W."
  },
  "exams": [
   "PUC"
  ],
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "tags": [
   "board-pattern"
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "1n01-q28",
  "chapterId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals",
  "topicId": "puc1-electronics-01-electric-circuit-fundamentals.t5",
  "stem": {
   "en": "A 24 V source in series with 6 Ω feeds terminals A–B, across which a 3 Ω resistor is connected. The Thevenin equivalent at A–B is:"
  },
  "options": [
   {
    "en": "8 V in series with 2 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "24 V in series with 9 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "16 V in series with 2 Ω"
   },
   {
    "en": "8 V in series with 9 Ω"
   }
  ],
